  "statement": "Mandatory pre-deployment audits for frontier artificial intelligence labs function primarily as regulatory moats that protect dominant market actors from open-source competition. When Congress enacted the Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002, fixed compliance costs skyrocketed, driving smaller accounting firms out of business and consolidating the audit market into the Big Four. Frontier artificial intelligence governance risks replicating this exact industrial exclusion under the banner of safety. Under the current proposals backed by large technology firms, smaller startups and open-source developers face compliance burdens they cannot absorb. Large cloud providers profit from these barriers by pricing out nimble rivals who lack legal and compliance teams of fifty people. The U.S. Artificial Intelligence Safety Institute holds no statutory mandate to crush decentralized software development in the name of risk mitigation. Lawmakers must recognize that high fixed compliance thresholds serve established corporate power rather than public protection.\n\nOpponents will argue that without strict pre-deployment gatekeeping, open-source frontier models will circulate without safety guardrails and create uncontrollable systemic risks. This objection rests on the unproven assumption that smaller open-source developers pose the same systemic hazard as capitalized enterprise labs with massive compute clusters. Historical precedent from the cybersecurity sector shows that mandated secure coding standards improve software resilience without requiring centralized federal certification for every minor code repository. Open-source communities mitigate vulnerabilities through distributed peer review, not through opaque bureaucratic checkpoints administered by political appointees. When the National Institute of Standards and Technology issued its cybersecurity framework in 2014, compliance was tiered rather than prohibitive. Policymakers should apply that same proportional logic to artificial intelligence by requiring audits only at verifiable compute and deployment thresholds, rather than treating all public code as an existential threat.\n\nFederal oversight should shift away from speculative existential scenarios and focus instead on verifiable present harms experienced by everyday citizens. Documented cases of algorithmic discrimination in hiring algorithms and automated benefits denial systems affect millions of Americans right now. The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau documented numerous instances in 2023 where opaque automated scoring models denied credit without lawful explanation. These are measurable administrative failures that demand immediate regulatory enforcement and civil monetary penalties under existing federal statutes. Frontier labs direct congressional attention toward hypothetical catastrophic risks precisely to distract lawmakers from these ongoing civil rights violations. Congress should fund enforcement agencies to penalize real harms today, rather than subsidizing corporate compliance monopolies to guard against science-fiction disasters tomorrow.",
