=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [acc-beliefs-067] Decentralized Markets and Open Development Outperform Central Control in Driving AI Progress: Nirvana Fallacy — The claim implicitly compares real-world centralized planning (with its genuine inefficiencies) against an idealized decentralized market rather than against actually-existing market structures, which themselves exhibit oligopolistic concentration in AI.
- [acc-beliefs-067] Decentralized Markets and Open Development Outperform Central Control in Driving AI Progress: Hasty Generalization — Extrapolating from open-source successes in software (Linux, Apache) to structural superiority across all AI development contexts — including safety-critical frontier research — is a generalization unsupported by systematic cross-domain evidence.
- [acc-intentions-071] Secure Early Access to Human-Generated Training Data as a Strategic Asset: Appeal to Scarcity — The urgency framing relies on asserting that human-generated data is a finite, dwindling resource, but this may be overstated — the internet continues to produce vast quantities of human content, and the true bottleneck may be curation and compute rather than raw data availability.
- [acc-intentions-100] Tie AI Governance Milestones to Empirical Compute Scaling Benchmarks: Proxy Fallacy (Misplaced Concreteness) — Treating compute as the definitive operationalization of AI capability conflates a measurable input metric with the complex emergent outputs governance actually needs to track; capability breakthroughs from architectural changes may not register in compute benchmarks.
- [acc-desires-026] Deploying AI to Run Society at Scale: Nirvana Fallacy — Advocates implicitly compare AI governance to an idealized optimum rather than to realistic, imperfect human alternatives, ignoring that AI systems bring their own failure modes, biases, and unintended consequences at scale.
- [acc-intentions-088] Govern AI Through Market Incentives and Transparent Telemetry: Nirvana Fallacy — The node implicitly compares an idealized, efficiently-functioning market governance model against a flawed or captured state regulatory apparatus, rather than comparing realistic versions of both approaches.
- [acc-intentions-088] Govern AI Through Market Incentives and Transparent Telemetry: Optimism Bias — The framework systematically underweights the documented tendency of self-regulatory regimes to produce weak standards, slow enforcement, and industry-favorable outcomes when public oversight is absent.
- [acc-intentions-082] Scale AI Agents from Pilots to Production: Hasty Generalization — Extrapolating from successful narrow pilot projects to full-scale autonomous enterprise deployment assumes that the controlled conditions of pilots are representative of the complexity, variance, and adversarial conditions of production environments — a leap not justified by current evidence.

